From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 14:26:52 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] venus: serve reference-counted layouts from the caller's
 allocator

vkCreateDescriptorSetLayout and vkCreatePipelineLayout ignored the
VkAllocationCallbacks the application passed and took the layout's storage
from the device's own allocator instead, because both layouts are reference
counted and can outlive their destroy command. An application that installs
callbacks therefore never sees the allocation its layout costs, and a layout
created while every one of its callbacks refuses memory still succeeds.

Vulkan asks an implementation to serve an object's host allocations from the
callbacks its create command carried, and the object management CTS observes
it:

    dEQP-VK.api.object_management.alloc_callback_fail.pipeline_layout_empty
    dEQP-VK.api.object_management.alloc_callback_fail.pipeline_layout_single
    dEQP-VK.api.object_management.alloc_callback_fail.descriptor_set_layout_empty
    dEQP-VK.api.object_management.alloc_callback_fail.descriptor_set_layout_single

create each layout with an allocator that fails its first allocation and
expect VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY; all four reported the QualityWarning
"Allocation callbacks not called" because construction succeeded with no
callback ever reached.

Serve both layouts from the creating command's callbacks, keep those
callbacks in the layout, and return the storage to them when the last
reference goes away. Reference counting is unchanged: the layout still
survives the descriptor sets, pipelines, update templates and push
descriptors that describe themselves with it, and its renderer object is
still destroyed with the last reference. The storage keeps the device
allocation scope that extended lifetime needs, so an application that pools
by scope pools it with the rest of the device's memory, and the callbacks a
destroy command carries must already be compatible with the ones its create
command carried.

vn_object_alloc.h holds the allocate-and-keep pair both layouts use, and the
vn_object_alloc test in the venus suite pins the contract: the creating
command's callbacks serve the storage and receive it back, absent callbacks
fall back to the device's own, and callbacks that refuse the allocation fail
the object instead of letting the device's allocator serve it quietly.
